I don't remember for sure, but I think the man page is wrong (the file must
exist and the host must be listed, or it's denied).

You can find out for sure by doing a

	/sbin/init.d/syslog stop

and then running

	/usr/sbin/syslogd -d

to run it in debugging mode.  Then try sending messages to it from the
firewall, or using `logger' from some other UNIX host.

BTW, in 3.x and some of 4.x, there was a bug in the start/stop script
for syslog -- if you ran the stop it would only stop syslog, whereas if
you ran the start it would start both syslog and binlog.  This means that
if you stop it manually and then later restart it using the script, you'll
end up with two copies of binlogd running.  Just kill one.

In any case, once you have syslog running in debug mode, it will tell you
if it even sees anything come in via the log socket.

