In the analyst presentations we saw Capellas talk a bit about the
strengths of a merged HP/Compaq. One slide showed "Superdome" and
"Himalaya" at the very high end, dropping down through examples of mid
range and low end. VMS did not appear on the slide anywhere. This
matches exactly the document produced by HP attacking Walter Hewlett.

At the high end "Superdome" is shown coming from HP and Himalaya from
Compaq. Under midrange servers we see "#2 market share" coming from HP
and nothing coming from Compaq. Next down is "entry level RISC" which
is shown as coming from HP and finally "Industry Standard Servers"
says "Market Leader" shown as coming from Compaq. There is no room for
VMS here.  See
ftp://ftp.compaq.com/pub/corporate/ir/Analyst2002_Capellas2.ppt slide
6

However they still managed to put up a slide during Elias'
presentation mentioning the dates for the VMS Itanium port. I find an
inconsistency here but one that could have a logical explanation.
Perhaps Compaq intend to complete the port (in fact realise that they
must) but then immediately sell VMS. Perhaps they already have a buyer
lined up? Intel? Management buyout massively subsidized by Compaq to
get it off their hands?

Could a worldwide sales team be put in place with the resources to
really push VMS?

And what should such an organization do about future platforms - tie
itself to the uncertain future of Itanium on the assumption that Intel
will make it a success or start looking at X86-64 given that even
Intel is hedging its bets here with Hammer compatibility mode
currently being designed into future IA32 chips.

