In article <e6c2d89d.0201290354.117dadd@posting.google.com>, QuarterHorseman@penvillage.com (Quarter Horseman) writes:

>Read the AdvFS Admin manual and pay specific attention to the
>"Checking Free Space and Disk Usage" section as well as the sections
>referred to by "quotacheck" entries in the manual's index.  All the
>manuals are online and publicly-available.

The problem with this bit of advise, is that it doesn't apply to the
du(1) command.  df(1) yes.  du(1) no.  As others noted the problem
is that the particular file is probably sparse; it doesn't have
blocks allocated to all the space it presents.  Such space reads
as zeros and when written allocates space.  Most file copying
utilities don't know how to detect the whole and simply fill them
in when the data is copied.

The difference in reporting is that du(1) adds up the number of
allocated blocks in files.  Since this doesn't have blocks allocated
for all the space, you see a difference.  du(1) also won't report
the space used by files that are open, but which don't have names.
