I think you are being unreasonably pessimistic about the situation.

Firstly, much of VMS is readily portable because it is in a high-level
language.  The real tasks are with Macro and other uncommon beasts.  The
effort involved with porting is becoming less and less, or maybe the
engineers know how to do it in their sleep. 

Second, I think a lot of the depressed sales and even defection from VMS
are a reaction to Compaq's  handling of it.  People who had hoped for
something of a revival under Compaq have now been disappointed.  Three
years and all they see is Compaq bailing out of Alpha and continuing
wasting money in the PC arena.  Compaq has shown that despite the
rhetoric at the time of the Digital buyout, they are not really
interested in anything but the (no)Win-toys.

Third, and importantly, I'm making no assumptions about VMS staying with
Compaq.  There is a lot of support here for IBM to become the
foster-parent for example.  Anyone who bought VMS can be assumed to be
enthusiastic and so having the option of moving to AMD could be a good
move.  (It also doesn't mean Intel *or* AMD but it could mean Intel
*AND* AMD.)

I take your point about customers not being happy with two transitions,
but if Itanic falls out before the customers start porting, then the
customers would just port once which they are going to do anyway.


John McL


