The at commands (and crontabs - also worth looking at) are recorded in 
files which you can find in:

/var/spool/cron/atjobs/USERNAME.JOBNUMBER
and
/var/spool/cron/crontabs/USERNAME

if you cat any of these files you'll see who's got jobs waiting to run 
and what those jobs are going to do.


The command history is kept in the users home directory in a file called 
.history so if you do something like:

find / -name .history -exec grep rm {} \;

it'll search for all the .history files and then grep for any "rm" 
commands, this may or may not be usefull as there are likely to be many 
legitimate rm commands and in order to work out if a particular "rm *" 
command caused your problem you'd have to work out from previous 
commands if the user had been working in a temporary/private directory 
when they issued the command, also they might not have removed the file, 
they could have moved it, e.g "mv * /tmp/tmp.file". Also if anyone was 
trying to be malicious and then cover their tracks they would remove the 
.history file after issuing the destructive commands in order to 
eliminate the evidence.



Best of luck.

