news:a2chr6$fs0$1@pulp.srv.ualberta.ca...
> In comp.os.linux.alpha Chris Adams <cmadams@hiwaay.net> wrote:
>
> > If you use UFS under Tru64, you should be able to read (haven't tried
> > write, but I think it should work) those partitions from Linux.  You
> > can't access AdvFS domains from Linux (that's a proprietary filesystem).
>
> Current kernels have FreeVxFS support from Veritas (makers of AdvFS).

AdvFS was written by Chris Saether and Debbie Girdler (and likely some
others) at DECWest, not by Veritas.

> How close is FreeVxFS to AdvFS I have no idea.  Probably not very.

Definitely not very:  AdvFS was designed to be a DCE-style 'local file
system', complete with 'aggregates, 'cloning', etc.  VxFS is a
log-protected, extent-based Unix file system with (AFAIK) no such DCE-style
capabilities (though its underlying VxVM logical volume manager has some
similar features and indeed IIRC is used by Tru64 underneath AdvFS).

- bill





