Pierre Villers wrote:

> I am looking for a debugger different of ladebug with the possibility to
> have graphic results.

That depends a lot on what you mean by "graphic results". If you mean 
simply that you want a GUI (window/mouse) interface rather than a character 
cell interface in a terminal window, the ladebug has it. On any relatively 
recent version, try "ladebug -gui", or use the "gui" command at a ladebug 
prompt. (And if your version is too old to handle that, upgrade; you can 
download the latest from the tru64unix.compaq.com development tools site.)

Many people also love Totalview, a 3rd party product of Etnus 
(www.etnus.com). It's "quirky", but powerful. While it does work in a 
"graphic" window, it formats that window to look much like a character cell 
terminal. Recent versions can be scripted using TCL in very powerful ways, 
and it can add inline "patches" to execute interpreted C-like code rather 
than just doing normal breakpoints. (There's also now a "command line" 
interface to Totalview, though you need to speak TCL, not some simplified 
command interface.) Totalview is considered a requirement in many 
parallel/distributed computing environments, though ladebug has come a long 
way towards catching up.

