Most shells support the TMOUT environment variable (or
others) that will log off an idle user that is at a
shell prompt.  But this is only for users at a shell
prompt, not in applications.

If you need a general solution, our company wrote/sells
a product called LOGMON.  This software monitors the
cpu usage of each user, and their child processes.  That
way we can be sure the user truly is idle.  We can then
log them off.  You can vary the inactivity time by user,
time of day, etc.  You can control the behaviour by
application also.  We support tru64 as well as almost
every other Unix variant.

