Hoo boy.  Is a microprocessor defined by its Instruction Set Architecture,
or by how it executes the ISA - its implementation?  CISC processors have
seldom executed instructions directly.  In the early 1980s time period, CISC
processors (the ones with CISC ISAs) were microprogrammed.  At this time,
CISC processors are implemented by mostly breaking complex instructions down
into simpler "RISC" instructions.

I hypothesize that if that makes current x86 micros "RISC" processors, then
there has _never_ been a CISC x86 processor, because the _implementation_ of
x86 processors has always involved breaking instructions down into a series
of simple steps.

Well, it's been a while since the RISC-CISC wars were fought in this NG.
What I'm asking is, "Has there _ever_ been a true CISC x86 processor?  If
so, why are current x86 processors not considered to be CISCs, aside from
the fact that there are a lot of former Taliban - excuse me, former RISC
supporters - out there who don't like the fact that they wound up on the
wrong side, as their worthless stock options conclusively prove?"

