> Guy Paulus <gpaulus@astro.ulb.ac.be> wrote:
> > After increasing the size of partition dsk0g (usr_domain),
> > I wanted to extend the AdvFS domain usr_domain to fill the
> > partition. I did the following:
>
> > * vdump of /usr
> > * rmfdmn of usr_domain
> > * mkfdmn of new usr_domain on /dev/disk/dsk0g
> > * mkfset of file sets usr and var
> > * mounting usr_domain#usr and usr_domain#var on their respective
> >    mount points

Before dumping, were /usr/and /var different file systems? If they were,
you're mostly screwed.

> > * vrestore of the dumped /usr.
>
> > Problem: /var is now empty. I have booted in single-user mode
> > and tried various things but to no avail. Is there a way to
> > recover /var without any backup, or is re-installing Tru64 5.1
> > the only way out?
>
> So usr_domain#usr is mounted on /usr and usr_domain#var is mounted on
> /var and, after restore the dump, /var is empty? That's expected
> because, before the dump /var -> usr/var. So, just copy /usr/var to
> /var, remove /usr/var, and you should be set.

When system installs with /var inside /usr, /var is made a symbolic link to
/usr/var. When you install it as separate file systems, /usr/var is a
sym-link to /var.

So, the only ray of hope for you is a situation where /var *was* in /usr and
you later made it a separate file system *without* deleting the old /usr/var

Nix.




