-my answer are from a 5.1A system, your output may vary -

Can you check your interfaces and routes :

# ifconfig -a
# netstat -nr

check ftp :


# grep ftp /etc/inetd.conf
ftp     stream  tcp     nowait  root    /usr/sbin/ftpd          ftpd
#tftp   dgram   udp     wait    root    /usr/sbin/tftpd      
# telnet localhost 21
Trying 127.0.0.1...
Connected to localhost.
Escape character is '^]'.
220 mickey.etc.vbe.cpqcorp.net FTP server (Compaq Tru64 UNIX Version
5.60) read.
QUIT
221 Goodbye.
Connection closed by foreign host.

Check you can lookup and reverse lookup the client address :
nslookup pcname.dom
nslookup xx.xx.xx.xx (pc address)

Check your client ftp does or does not use any ftp proxy. in this case
you will see the proxy address in your ftp session

check this by netstat -n | grep '\.2[0-1] '
tcp connection on port 20 and 21
try to reverse lookup the incoming address

Can you check your Ethernet physical link as well. I had this behavior
when using autonegotiated and negotiation was not so efficient.

Try to force at console level your ewa0_mode or whatever to the actual
value accepted by the switch in front of it. If you don't know the
syntax, just type
>>> set ewa0_mode zzz
and it will list you the valid options. Use full duplex only if your
switch accepts it, and specially do not use it if you have a hub. Fix
the speed as needed.

If this does  not change anything, I do not know if this is convenient
for your server or not, but try to plug a crossover cable to a PC,
with an address in the same subnet, to isolate any network equipment.
That will tell you if it is a software issue or a network path
problem.

Hope this help,


